# Line-ending policy for the repo.
#
# Without this file, `git config core.autocrlf true` (the default on
# Windows) would re-introduce CRLF in any shell script that gets
# authored or edited on Windows. That is harmless for Python / Node /
# SQL (the interpreters don't care), but it silently breaks Linux-side
# shell scripts: bash sees `set -euo pipefail\r` and aborts with
# `set: pipefail: invalid option name`. The exact failure mode we hit
# on db/migrate.sh — the bash `set` builtin reads `\r` as part of the
# option name and the script never runs.
#
# `text=auto` makes git normalise line endings on commit/checkout
# based on a content heuristic — it's the default for most files.
# For files that MUST stay LF (scripts that run inside Linux
# containers), we pin `text eol=lf` so `core.autocrlf=true` is
# overruled for those paths.
#
# See: https://docs.github.com/en/get-started/getting-started-with-git/configuring-git-to-handle-line-endings

*               text=auto

# Shell scripts and Dockerfiles run inside Linux containers — they
# MUST be LF regardless of the host OS or `core.autocrlf`.
*.sh            text eol=lf
*.bash          text eol=lf
Dockerfile      text eol=lf
**/Dockerfile   text eol=lf
*.dockerfile    text eol=lf

# PowerShell scripts only run on Windows, so CRLF is the safe default.
*.ps1           text eol=crlf
*.psm1          text eol=crlf
